    <desc>Open, close, reorder, pin and name tabs, and put the tabs on any side of the window.</desc>
  </info>

  <title>Use tabs</title>

<p>Tabs let you keep several terminals in one window. Click a tab to switch
  to it.</p>

  <section id="add-tab">
    <title>Open a new tab</title>
    <p>Press <keyseq><key>Shift</key><key>Ctrl</key><key>T</key></keyseq>, or click the
    <gui style="button">New Tab</gui> button at the left of the header bar.
    Click the arrow next to it to open the tab with another profile.</p>
    <p>New tabs are added after the last tab. To add them next to the current
    tab instead, open the <gui>Preferences</gui> and set <gui>New Tab
    Position</gui> in the <gui>Behavior</gui> group to <gui>Next</gui>.</p>
  </section>

  <section id="close-tab">
    <title>Close tabs</title>
    <p>Press <keyseq><key>Shift</key><key>Ctrl</key><key>W</key></keyseq>, click the
    <gui style="button">×</gui> on the tab, or right-click the tab and select
    <gui style="menuitem">Close Terminal</gui>.</p>
    <p>To close all tabs but one, right-click it and select
    <gui style="menuitem">Close Other Terminals</gui>. Pinned tabs are kept.
    If programs still run in tabs that you close, <app>Pano Terminal</app>
    asks first.</p>
  </section>

  <section id="reopen-tab">
    <title>Reopen a closed tab</title>
    <p>Press <keyseq><key>Shift</key><key>Ctrl</key><key>Alt</key><key>T</key></keyseq>, select
    <gui style="menuitem">Reopen Closed Tab</gui> in the main menu, or click
    <gui style="button">Undo</gui> in the notice that appears after closing a
    tab. The tab comes back where it was, with its panes, their folders and
    their profiles, but with new shells. The last ten closed tabs are kept,
    also those of windows that were closed.</p>
  </section>

  <section id="reorder-tab">
    <title>Reorder tabs</title>
    <p>Drag a tab to another place among the tabs. You can also right-click a
    tab and select <gui style="menuitem">Move Terminal Left</gui> or
    <gui style="menuitem">Move Terminal Right</gui>, or press
    <keyseq><key>Shift</key><key>Ctrl</key><key>Page Up</key></keyseq> and <keyseq><key>Shift</key><key>Ctrl</key><key>Page Down</key></keyseq>.</p>
    <p>Drag a tab onto another <app>Pano Terminal</app> window to move it
    there, or out of the window to give it a window of its own. Right-click a
    tab and select <gui style="menuitem">Detach Terminal</gui> to do the same
    without dragging. Drop a tab onto a terminal of another tab to make the two
    <link xref="gs-split-panes">panes</link> of one tab.</p>
  </section>

  <section id="switch-tab">
    <title>Switch between tabs</title>
    <p>Press <keyseq><key>Ctrl</key><key>Page Up</key></keyseq> and <keyseq><key>Ctrl</key><key>Page Down</key></keyseq> to go to
    the previous and the next tab, and <keyseq><key>Alt</key><key>1</key></keyseq> to
    <keyseq><key>Alt</key><key>0</key></keyseq> to go to the first ten tabs. Click the
    <gui style="button">Show Open Tabs</gui> button in the header bar to see
    all tabs at once.</p>
  </section>

  <section id="pin-tab">
    <title>Pin tabs</title>
    <p>Right-click a tab and select <gui style="menuitem">Pin Terminal</gui> to
    keep it at the start of the tabs. Pinned tabs are not closed by
    <gui style="menuitem">Close Other Terminals</gui>.</p>
  </section>

  <section id="rename-tab">
    <title>Name a tab</title>
    <p>Programs such as the shell set the title of their terminal, which the
    tab shows. To give a terminal a title of your own, right-click its tab or
    open the main menu and select <gui style="menuitem">Set Title…</gui>.
    Programs cannot change this title, and it is kept when the
    <link xref="gs-session">session is restored</link>. Leave it empty to
    show the title of the program again.</p>
    <p>To ignore the titles that programs set in every terminal, open the
    <gui>Preferences</gui> and turn on <gui>Ignore Titles Set by Programs</gui>
    in the <gui>Behavior</gui> group. Terminals then show their working
    folder, unless you gave them a title.</p>
  </section>

  <section id="tab-position">
    <title>Put the tabs on another side</title>
    <p>To show the tabs at the bottom, left or right of the window:</p>
    <steps>
<item><p>Press the menu button in the top-right corner of the window and select <gui style="menuitem">Preferences</gui>.</p></item>
<item><p>In the <gui>Appearance</gui> group, set <gui>Tab Bar Position</gui>.</p></item>
</steps>
    <p>On the left and right, the tabs form a list, with the pinned tabs at the
    top. Drag the inner edge of the list to make it wider or narrower. Press
    <keyseq><key>Shift</key><key>Ctrl</key><key>L</key></keyseq> to move the focus to the list: the arrow keys
    then switch tabs, the menu key opens the menu of a tab, <key>Delete</key>
    closes it, and <key>Enter</key> or <key>Esc</key> go back to the
    terminal.</p>
  </section>

  <section id="middle-click">
    <title>Middle-click on a tab</title>
    <p>A middle click on a tab does nothing by default. To make it close the
    tab, or paste the selected text into it, open the <gui>Preferences</gui>
    and set <gui>Middle-Click on a Tab</gui> in the <gui>Behavior</gui>
    group.</p>
  </section>

